WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:11.098
Every machine needs ten different tools. One for windows, one for screenshots, one for packages... and a different set on every OS.

00:00:11.449 --> 00:00:20.796
And your AI agent? It can write the script — but it can't see the screen, click a button, or show you where to look.

00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:30.521
So what if one small binary could do all of it — on every machine — and your agent could just... use it?

00:00:30.871 --> 00:00:38.375
This is aos. First thing it does: check this machine can actually keep its promises.
$ aos doctor

00:00:38.725 --> 00:00:42.597
What's open, and on which screen? One command each.
$ aos window list --app=TextEdit

00:00:42.597 --> 00:00:43.706
What's open, and on which screen? One command each.
$ aos display list

00:00:44.056 --> 00:00:49.884
Now, put things where they belong. TextEdit top right, Calculator bottom right — no dragging.
$ aos window move TextEdit --monitor=2 --zone=2B

00:00:49.884 --> 00:00:52.283
Now, put things where they belong. TextEdit top right, Calculator bottom right — no dragging.
$ aos window move Calculator --monitor=2 --zone=2D

00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:58.299
Save the whole layout once... scatter everything... and bring it all back in a single pass.
$ aos window save /tmp/aosdemo/work.json --monitor=2

00:00:58.299 --> 00:01:01.910
Save the whole layout once... scatter everything... and bring it all back in a single pass.
$ aos window move TextEdit --monitor=2 --zone=1B

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.680
Save the whole layout once... scatter everything... and bring it all back in a single pass.
$ aos window arrange /tmp/aosdemo/work.json

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.369
It launches apps, and waits until the window is really there.
$ aos launch Calculator --wait

00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:15.016
It types — straight into the window you name, never the wrong one.
$ aos key type " Typed by aos." --app=TextEdit

00:01:15.016 --> 00:01:16.334
It types — straight into the window you name, never the wrong one.
$ aos key press cmd+left --app=TextEdit

00:01:16.684 --> 00:01:22.634
It sees, too. A screenshot of just one window...
$ aos capture screenshot --app=Calculator --out=/tmp/aosdemo/calc.png

00:01:22.634 --> 00:01:23.682
It sees, too. A screenshot of just one window...
$ aos file stat /tmp/aosdemo/calc.png

00:01:24.032 --> 00:01:30.969
Now the good part. Ask where the seven is — by name, not by pixels...
$ aos find Calculator --text=7 --role=button --exact

00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:34.000
...and click it.
$ aos mouse click 2722 751

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:43.021
Or show a human where to click. A big, friendly arrow, right on top of everything.
$ aos draw Calculator --find=7 --role=button --exact --text="press 7" --color=green --duration=6

00:01:43.371 --> 00:01:51.534
Point at a menu by name, and it boxes exactly that.
$ aos draw TextEdit --find=Format --role=menuitem --text="Format menu" --duration=5

00:01:51.884 --> 00:01:58.128
And it can narrate what an agent is doing — on screen, without stealing focus.
$ aos subtitle show "agent: tidying your desktop" --seconds=4 --position=bottom

00:01:58.478 --> 00:02:03.871
Files work the same everywhere. Make a folder, write a note, read it back.
$ aos file mkdir /tmp/aosdemo/notes

00:02:03.871 --> 00:02:07.065
Files work the same everywhere. Make a folder, write a note, read it back.
$ aos file write /tmp/aosdemo/notes/todo.txt "ship the demo"

00:02:07.065 --> 00:02:10.581
Files work the same everywhere. Make a folder, write a note, read it back.
$ aos file list /tmp/aosdemo/notes

00:02:10.581 --> 00:02:11.613
Files work the same everywhere. Make a folder, write a note, read it back.
$ aos file read /tmp/aosdemo/notes/todo.txt

00:02:11.963 --> 00:02:17.061
Run anything, and get the output back as clean JSON.
$ aos exec capture -- git --version

00:02:17.411 --> 00:02:20.895
One set of package verbs, whatever the OS calls its package manager.
$ aos pkg manager

00:02:20.895 --> 00:02:22.788
One set of package verbs, whatever the OS calls its package manager.
$ aos pkg search ripgrep

00:02:23.138 --> 00:02:26.388
Network, sound, power — the boring stuff, finally the same everywhere.
$ aos network ip

00:02:26.388 --> 00:02:29.139
Network, sound, power — the boring stuff, finally the same everywhere.
$ aos audio volume

00:02:29.139 --> 00:02:31.848
Network, sound, power — the boring stuff, finally the same everywhere.
$ aos power status

00:02:31.848 --> 00:02:32.885
Network, sound, power — the boring stuff, finally the same everywhere.
$ aos battery status

00:02:33.235 --> 00:02:37.387
The clipboard, in and out.
$ aos clipboard copy "hello from aos"

00:02:37.387 --> 00:02:38.440
The clipboard, in and out.
$ aos clipboard paste

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.098
Find a process, and stop it.
$ sleep 600 &

00:02:42.098 --> 00:02:44.971
Find a process, and stop it.
$ aos process find sleep

00:02:44.971 --> 00:02:46.021
Find a process, and stop it.
$ aos process kill sleep

00:02:46.371 --> 00:02:50.773
Turn any command into a real background service — and clean it up just as easily.
$ aos service create aosdemo-nap -- /bin/sleep 60

00:02:50.773 --> 00:02:53.923
Turn any command into a real background service — and clean it up just as easily.
$ aos service list

00:02:53.923 --> 00:02:54.954
Turn any command into a real background service — and clean it up just as easily.
$ aos service remove aosdemo-nap

00:02:55.304 --> 00:03:00.264
Watch the machine change, one event per line.
$ aos watch file /tmp/aosdemo --max=1

00:03:00.614 --> 00:03:09.531
The dangerous ones — lock, sleep, restart — are there too. We'll just read the label, not press the button.
$ aos system --help

00:03:09.881 --> 00:03:17.105
Open any site as its own clean app window.
$ aos webapp open https://muthuishere.github.io/aos/

00:03:17.455 --> 00:03:28.359
Need a second pair of eyes? Share this screen to any browser on your network.
$ aos remote share --monitor=2

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:35.869
And every one of these commands becomes a tool any agent can call, over MCP.
$ aos serve tools --groups=window

00:03:36.219 --> 00:03:40.136
It even remembers what it was asked to do.
$ aos obs summary

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:46.540
And the agent skill installs itself — so your agent already knows all of this.
$ aos skill path

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:52.668
Which brings us to the fun part. Just... ask.

00:03:53.018 --> 00:04:13.748
I'll say it like I'd say it to a friend. TextEdit top right, Calculator bottom right.
you: put TextEdit in the top-right quarter and Calculator in the bottom-right quarter of my second monitor

00:04:14.098 --> 00:04:28.458
Point at the seven, with a green arrow.
you: point at the 7 button on Calculator with a green arrow saying press 7

00:04:28.808 --> 00:04:51.138
Find the Format menu, box it — and tell me when you're done.
you: find the Format menu in TextEdit and box it, then show a subtitle saying done

00:04:51.488 --> 00:05:01.288
And clean up after yourself.
you: clean up the drawings

00:05:01.638 --> 00:05:31.054
And it works from a script, or CI, too. One line, no chat window — and the desktop just follows along.
$ claude -p "put TextEdit on the left half of monitor 2 and point at its Format menu" --dangerously-skip-permissions

00:05:31.404 --> 00:05:39.858
One binary. Every machine. Go make your computer work for you.
